body:
> Hello @FortressNebula, could you be more specific on what effects you wish to achieve with this?

For example, when using a palette shader I would want the light sources to illuminate an area according to the colour gradient in the palette. That could mean the actual `LIGHT` value returned for each light would have to be different to follow the colour palette, which isn't possible otherwise. To illustrate the example, 
<img width="1184" height="717" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/40032bb7-9e29-455b-bb52-a503784bda8d" />
```
void light() {
  vec3 oldCol = COLOR.rgb;
  float cNdotL = max(0.05, dot(NORMAL, LIGHT_DIRECTION));
  float additionalIntensity = 1.;
  if (!useNormals) cNdotL = 1.;
  vec4 brightness = vec4(LIGHT_COLOR.rgb * oldCol.rgb * LIGHT_ENERGY * cNdotL * intensity, 1);
  vec4 newCol = min(vec4(oldCol.rgb + brightness.rgb, 1.), vec4(1., 1., 1., 1.));

  // Cycle through every palette color and see which is closest
  int index = -1;
  float lowestError = 100.0;

  for (int i = 0; i < numPaletteColors; i++) {
	float err = sum(abs(newCol.rgb - texture(paletteTexture, vec2((float(i)+0.5) / float(numPaletteColors), 0.5)).rgb));
	if (err < lowestError) {
		lowestError = err;
		index = i;
	}
  }

  vec4 col = texture(paletteTexture, vec2((float(index)+0.5) / float(numPaletteColors), 0.5));
  LIGHT = vec4(col.rgb - oldCol.rgb + brightness.rgb*vec3(.1), 1);
}
```
This area is lit with two sprites with the above shader, but because the light sources interfere with each other it causes the bits in the middle to be pushed to really high saturations and stray from the intended look.
By changing `oldCol` to use `ACCUMULATED_COLOR.rgb`, it looks like this:
<img width="812" height="656" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c553900a-2a11-4c94-8a2b-5a19d797b822" />
With everything following the colour palette. Without the built-in, there would be completely no way to have different light sources provide different light values to the scene that way.
